Malted barley is part-germinated barley that has been killed by being
heat-dried and/or roasted.  Most of the sugar for making the alcohol
comes from "pale" malt, which is mostly just dried, while the darker
malts (crystal, amber, brown, chocolate, "patent black") are used to add
flavour, colour, and body (they have much less fermentable sugars, but
contain interesting dextrins and suchlike).

>>while beer has hops in it. So when he refered to the beer style I think 
>>he was refering to adding of hops to the brewing process.
>
>Err, no; that's not the distinction.  Both ales and lagers are beer.
>Hops have only been used for a few centuries, and lagers are only a few
>centuries old. (nettles, among other things, used to be used0.

Extra data: Originally, "ale" was "fermented malt drink, probably with
flavourings" and "beer" was "ale flavoured with hops".

>Ale uses a "top-fermenting" yeast; it's more active up at the top.
>Lagers use a "bottom-fermenting" yeast, which mutated from the centuries
>ago, and works better at colder temperatures.  After fermentation,
>lagering, or storing, at cold temperatures is used to finish the beer.
>
>Both, however, are beer (at least in english).

Indeed.  Although a great deal of that which is legally beer does not
morally deserve to be called beer.  I favour an Australian friend's
suggestion of calling it "B22R". (Like B33R, but not as good.)

